<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Malwa Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1392 CE – 1562 CE · capital Mandu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A central Indian sultanate whose hilltop capital at Mandu is famed for its palaces, mosques and the romance of Baz Bahadur and Rupmati.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilawar-khan-ghuri">Dilawar Khan Ghuri</a>, appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluqs</a> in 1392, ruled independently after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi-1398">sack of Delhi</a> in 1398 and formally declared independence around 1401. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah">Hoshang Shah</a> moved the capital from Dhar to the plateau fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>, which he fortified and adorned.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a>, who seized the throne in 1436 and founded the Khalji line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, made it a major power, fighting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/udaipur-state">Mewar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a>; he built a victory tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandu">Mandu</a>. Mandu's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahaz-mahal">Jahaz Mahal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindola-mahal">Hindola Mahal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-mandu">Jama Masjid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoshang-shah">Hoshang Shah's</a> marble tomb show a distinctive Afghan style. The plateau's wealth came from agriculture and trade routes to Gujarat.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in 1531, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> later took it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baz-bahadur">Baz Bahadur</a>, the last independent ruler, was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar's</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adham-khan">Adham Khan</a> in 1561, and Malwa was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> by 1562.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/malwa-sultanate.jpg" alt="Malwa Sultanate" width="320" height="297"><figcaption>Image: Beylarbey · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghurids_of_the_Malwa_Sultanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1392 CE – 1562 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mandu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hoshang Shah, Mahmud Khalji, Baz Bahadur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Malwa, Ghuri and Khalji sultans of Malwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1477&amp;focus=malwa-sultanate" aria-label="Malwa Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6699 1353 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Malwa Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7019 1609L7018 1385M7019 1609L6939 1584M7019 1609L7018 1385"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M6939 1584h0M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7019 1609h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7019 1609h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mandu. <a href="/atlas?year=1477&amp;focus=malwa-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
